app.from:
lines.push(`Nearest substation at 33 kV or above, ${hit.k.toFixed(2)} km`
  + (hit.n ? ` \u2014 ${hit.n}` : "")
  + (hit.v && hit.v.length ? ` (${hit.v.join("/")} kV)` : ""));

app.to:
// A substation the layer has not named printed as a bare distance with the
// name clause silently dropped, which reads as a truncation rather than as
// a fact about the layer. Counted on this cut: of the 3,047 nearest
// substations, 2,333 carry a name tag and 714 do not. Across the 4,737
// distinct substations the fleet can reach, 1,154 are unnamed, and the gap
// is not confined to the small stuff -- 34 of the 251 mapped 400 kV sites
// have no name either.
//
// The name is not filled in. A settlement recogniser runs at 98.3% on this
// estate and its one failure was Rampton -> Rampson: plausible, wrong, and
// uncheckable by the reader it was written for. An empty field a reader can
// see is empty beats a field they cannot audit. Say the layer has no name.
lines.push(`Nearest substation at 33 kV or above, ${hit.k.toFixed(2)} km`
  + (hit.n ? ` \u2014 ${hit.n}` : " \u2014 unnamed in the mapped layer")
  + (hit.v && hit.v.length ? ` (${hit.v.join("/")} kV)` : ""));
if (!hit.n) lines.push("The layer carries this substation as geometry with no name tag: 714 of the 3,047 nearest substations in this cut arrive that way, and 34 of the 251 mapped 400 kV sites. No name has been inferred for it, because a plausible wrong name in a field a reader trusts cannot be checked by the reader it is written for.");
